## Authoring an ngn theme part

A theme part styles one control. Author it with `createThemePart` from `@ngneers/controls-themes/api`:

```ts
import { createThemePart, css } from '@ngneers/controls-themes/api';
import { selectControlTemplate } from '@ngneers/controls-themes/templates/select';

export const selectStyles = createThemePart({
  controlTemplate: selectControlTemplate,
  base: baseStyles.select,        // optional: build on the structural base part
  dependencies: [colorsTemplate], // variable/part templates this css uses
  root: {
    css: ({ v, c, d }) => css`
      ${c('input')} { gap: ${v('size.padding.sm')}; }
      ${c('icon')} { color: ${v('color.surface.500')}; }
    `,
  },
});
```

### The three helpers

- `c('<className>')` — target one of THIS control's own class names (see `get_control_theme`).
- `d('<depScope>', '<className>')` — target a class of a dependency control (e.g. `d('popover', 'content')`). Dependencies come from the control template.
- `v('<token>')` — read a theme token (see the token scopes below).

### Keys

- `root` — styles applied always. Add a `dark` key for dark-mode overrides.
- Each part accepts `{ css: ({v,c,d}) => css`...` }`.

### Gotchas

- The Nova palette **reverses shades in dark mode** (e.g. `surface.800` becomes light). For components that must stay dark in both schemes, use the raw grey color; the `500` shade is scheme-stable.
- Style through tokens (`v('color.…')`), never hardcode colors — that is what keeps light/dark and re-theming working.
- A brand-new theme part needs an empty `package.json` marker in its folder and a themes build before it resolves.
